Well, it took me about *four* years to come from the combat-wombat 
romping-stomping read-anything quaff-anything dont-care-about-pet 
wonder-why-I'm-dead way of playing to a more thought- and careful 
approach.  [Granted, I didn't really read spoilers that time, and 
just played for a quick fun.]  Still it took me half a year to 
ascend first time after that point (partially because I wanted to 
do with a healer first..).  Though I only seldom play now (once-twice 
a month, usually), and still have impressively stupid YASDs, I 
can do some conduct-ascensions with little sweat.  Don't worry, it 
just grows over time.  The point is to accept that nethack is not 
complicated, it's just complex.  The goal is to survive, and in the 
early game this means a) not to die by HP loss, b) not to starve to 
death.  In the early game, little else matters.  For both issues, 
there are some simple tactics and strategies detailed in the spoilers 
to be found on Eva Myer's page, but simply put, you want food, you 
want speed, you want a better (much better sometimes) AC, you want a 
good average damage, good combat tactics and everything else is way 
down the line (I wouldn't say no to reflection or MR, of course ;-)).  
The most important art one should learn is the art of running away.  
It is very useful to try to play a pacifist conduct -- not that you're 
likely to ascend one, but while trying to keep the conduct you'll learn 
how to interact efficiently with your pet, how to protect it from stupid 
ideas (come back, Rex, the watch-captain's our friend!  Ouch.. ), 
and how to outrun and -wit monsters.  And you will be fighting so 
hard for foodstuff that a usual game will seem smooth in comparison.. 

